<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/undated">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crusade</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A crusade was a war sanctioned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, typically against Muslims or other perceived enemies of the Church, in which participants received spiritual privileges. The crusades in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> brought conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, gravely damaging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, the crusade of 1212 defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Las Navas de Tolosa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/crusade.jpg" alt="Crusade" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Combat_deuxi%C3%A8me_croisade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
